{"index":312,"node_id":"60afdd48-be38-4069-9b68-68e03c93cd11","parent":"e37f476d-b604-48cc-93c9-d9e607656be9","role":"assistant","author_name":null,"create_time":1791069462.302838,"update_time":1791081829.99956,"content_type":"text","status":"finished_successfully","end_turn":true,"recipient":"all","model_slug":"gpt-5-6-thinking","default_model_slug":"gpt-5-6-thinking","part":1,"parts_total":1,"text":"I have now read the **entire current `GeminiSupervisor/README.md` sequentially**, from line 1 through EOF, rather than searching it. The file ends before line 1201. fileciteturn59file0 fileciteturn60file0 fileciteturn61file0 fileciteturn62file0 fileciteturn63file0 fileciteturn64file0 fileciteturn65file0\n\nAnd I see the distinction I was missing.\n\nThe README does **not** describe the flattening scheme as “turn a document into atomic lines/fragments,” nor is the damage/repair challenge itself the flattening scheme.\n\nThe scheme it establishes is this:\n\n```text\nnative canonical information\n        ↓\nstable canonical projection\n        ↓\nnormalized reading surface\n```\n\nThe critical property is **representation independence**: the native source can have one representation, while the stable reader can have another. What matters is that the stable reader is a **deterministic ordered JSON projection mechanically derived from the canonical source**, with provenance binding the projection back to that exact source. fileciteturn64file0\n\nSo “flattening” here means that the worker is not expected to reason through each source's native storage/interface/format. The information from that source is projected onto a **common, deterministic reading surface**. The system can therefore put heterogeneous informational sources into the same acquisition/verification mechanism.\n\nThe README then separates three layers:\n\n```text\n1. Canonical source\n   The authoritative native information.\n\n2. Stable reader\n   Deterministic ordered JSON projection of that source.\n   This is the normalized information surface.\n\n3. Nonce-scoped challenge copies\n   FULL   = intact instance of the stable reader\n   REPAIR = damaged instance of the same stable reader\n```\n\nThe third layer tests acquisition; it is **not what performs the informational flattening**. fileciteturn64file0\n\nAlso important: the README does **not actually specify the field-by-field projection algorithm**—i.e. exactly how Markdown, JSON, or another native format is decomposed into the ordered JSON records. It delegates that mechanical construction to `sync-stable-readers.js`. What the README specifies is the architectural flattening contract: **canonical heterogeneous representation → deterministic normalized ordered JSON reading representation**, preserving provenance. fileciteturn64file0\n\nSo if by “the informational flattening scheme” you mean the **actual record-construction algorithm**, I should not pretend the README gave me that algorithm. It doesn't. The next thing to read is `sync-stable-readers.js`, sequentially, because that is where the concrete projection operation lives."}
